{"id":"20260830T004947.369620329Z-2d8dac9d","time":"2026-08-30T00:49:47.369620329Z","session_id":"session-20260830T002012.706816133Z","task_id":"task-1788049212707426532","source":"claude","kind":"output","summary":"{\"type\":\"assistant\",\"message\":{\"model\":\"claude-opus-5\",\"id\":\"msg_011CeY1nWqeEuDjbXWST6yrP\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"thinking\",\"thinking\":\"\",\"signature\":\"\"}],\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"stop_details\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":2,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":315,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":136406,\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":0,\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":315},\"output_tokens\":2,\"service_tier\":\"standard\",\"inference_geo\":\"not_available\"},\"diagnostics\":null,\"context_management\":null},\"parent_tool_use_id\":null,\"session_id\":\"55d34540-5161-4344-aa41-f2d3346381f6\",\"uuid\":\"8eef2846-a092-49a3-bd25-09cc817ad5fb\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-08-30T00:49:47.369Z\",\"request_id\":\"req_011CeY1nVsMLat1hoqWBcpBL\"}","payload":{"stream":"stdout"}}
{"id":"20260830T005011.219539005Z-5f6ed8b1","time":"2026-08-30T00:50:11.219539005Z","session_id":"session-20260830T002012.706816133Z","task_id":"task-1788049212707426532","source":"claude","kind":"output","summary":"{\"type\":\"assistant\",\"message\":{\"model\":\"claude-opus-5\",\"id\":\"msg_011CeY1nWqeEuDjbXWST6yrP\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Done. `go test ./...` exits 0 across 37 packages; `gofmt -l cmd internal` and `go vet ./...` are clean; the worktree is committed at `39b1aa6a92cc80f61e6760a625275b5cd0acdd3e`.\\n\\n## [f1] blocking — copies and type changes no longer refuse a valid candidate\\n\\nI reproduced the finding against the cycle-1 code before fixing it. A real Git worktree with `diff.renames=copies` and a symlink replacement produced exactly the refusal the reviewer predicted:\\n\\n```\\n\\\"dup.txt\\\": Git reports it copied from \\\"src.txt\\\", the diff does not report it at all;\\n\\\"tc.txt\\\": Git reports type changed, the diff reports deleted and added\\n```\\n\\n**Copies** — `report.Status` gains `Copied`, and `FromDiff` parses `copy from`/`copy to`. This was a true blind spot, not just a reconciliation mismatch: a copy header names *two different paths* and at full similarity carries no `+++` line at all, so the renderer previously reported nothing for that record — the architect was told the candidate had not touched a file it had just created, and the reviewer never saw it.\\n\\n**Type changes** — I did **not** collapse these into a `TypeChanged` report status, which is where I depart from the reviewer's literal remedy. Collapsing would have silently removed the removal half of the change from two existing consumers: `routine.go`'s categorical \\\"the candidate deletes a test\\\" exclusion, and `testedit.go`'s created/deleted refutations. Repairing `routine.go` would mean editing a file the plan does not name — which this very candidate exists to refuse. So `FromDiff` keeps reporting the diff in the diff's own terms (deletion + creation), and `reconcileChangeSet` knows that pair *is* Git's one `T` record. The pair is required: half a type change is still a disagreement. `gitx.Unmerged` has no counterpart and is not given a dead one — it arises only from a base-less diff, and `candidate.Identity` refuses to load without a pinned `BaseSHA`, so it cannot reach either call site.\\n\\nTwo small judgement calls that follow from representing copies — flag them if you disagree:\\n- `Counts()` counts a copy destination as *added*; it did not exist before the change.\\n- `diffFileStates` treats a copy destination as *created*, so a grant to **edit** a file the candidate copied into place is refuted rather than inspected against facts pinned for a different file.\\n\\nNew tests build real repositories and read real `git diff` output (`report_test.go` copy/type-change/mode-change, `gitx` `Copied`/`TypeChanged`, `engine_test.go` end-to-end non-refusal). I verified they are non-vacuous by reverting only the fix and watching them fail.\\n\\n## [f2] major — revision materialized, audit partially\\n\\nThe candidate revision now exists: `39b1aa6a92cc80f61e6760a625275b5cd0acdd3e` (tree `202e5598…`), worktree clean. `sensei_workspace_status` resolves it with `revision_status: resolved`. I could not produce `9571c126451a` — that SHA is from a prior cycle and never existed in this checkout.\\n\\nRevision-bound evidence I could produce, where Sensei reads the diff from the revision range itself rather than from a payload I hand it:\\n- `sensei gate --diff HEAD~1..HEAD` — **0 would-block, 0 warn**. It needed three runs: each run had a different one-to-three files fail with `[scope] rpc error: code = DeadlineExceeded desc = stream terminated by RST_STREAM with error code: CANCEL`. Every one of the 7 files evaluated clean in at least two of the three runs; no file ever produced a finding.\\n- `awareness_preflight` over the 7 changed files: `CONFIDENCE_HIGH`, coverage 7/7 anchors sufficient, `ARCHITECTURE_SENSITIVE`, blast local, approval none. It names `sensei_code.candidate.reviewer_sees_every_changed_file` (critical) — the invariant f1 was about. All 22 required tests it lists pass, run by name.\\n- `sensei impact-gate` is not usable here: `cannot resolve services repo; pass -services-repo`.\\n\\n**What I could not do:** re-run `awareness_audit_diff` against the revision. It has no CLI subcommand and no `AuditDiff` RPC on the awareness server (`grpcurl list` shows Briefing, EditCheck, Impact, Metadata, Preflight, Propose, Query, ReferenceSites, Resolve, and no audit method), so the only route is the MCP tool with a 68 KB diff inline. Hand-transcribing that risks corrupting it into `malformed_diff` — the exact failure this repo already has a scar for. The revision now exists, so anyone can regenerate the exact bytes with `git diff --no-ext-diff HEAD~1 HEAD --` and pass them with `expected_head=39b1aa6a92cc80f61e6760a625275b5cd0acdd3e`. I left that undone rather than submit evidence I could not vouch for.\\n\\nI also did not run `sensei propose` to record the f1 failure class, though it is a durable one worth keeping — it would stage `docs/awareness/*.yaml`, a file the plan does not name. Your call.\\n\\nAWG: briefing(internal/report/report.go, internal/workflow/engine.go) | invariants: candidate.reviewer_sees_every_changed_file, roles.adversarial_roles_inherit_nothing_and_bind_to_a_revision | uncertainty: the revision-bound diff audit is unreachable without an inline payload, and the type-change modelling departs from the reviewer's literal remedy to stay inside the plan\"}],\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"stop_details\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":2,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":315,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":136406,\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":0,\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":315},\"output_tokens\":2,\"service_tier\":\"standard\",\"inference_geo\":\"not_available\"},\"diagnostics\":null,\"context_management\":null},\"parent_tool_use_id\":null,\"session_id\":\"55d34540-5161-4344-aa41-f2d3346381f6\",\"uuid\":\"2a1bb1a4-5d2b-429f-938f-948c916f92a7\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-08-30T00:50:11.217Z\",\"request_id\":\"req_011CeY1nVsMLat1hoqWBcpBL\"}","payload":{"stream":"stdout"}}
